 { White Blocks the Queenside and Wins on the Kingside The position in the diagram occurred in the famous Karpov – Unzicker game from the Nice Olympiad of 1974. Since this example is well-known to many, I will present it without annotations. The most important thing to observe is the position in the diagram. White has managed to block the queenside in a very original way: by placing the bishop on a7. In contrast to simply closing the queenside with pawns, having this bishop on a7 means White can re-open the file whenever he wants. Nevertheless, this will not be necessary, as his kingside play will decide the game. Here, Karpov proceeded with: } 30. f4 $1 f6 ( { PH: } 30... exf4 31. Nxf4 { at first looks tempting for Black, but how is he going to get a knight to e5? And even if a knight gets there, White will exchange it with Ng4 or Nf3. } ) 
31. f5 g5 ( { PH: More resilient would have been } 31... Nf7 32. Ng3 Bh6 33. Qf2 Kh8 { though White is still better after } 34. h4 $5 { ± , with ideas of taking fxg6 followed by h5, gaining the f5-square for a knight. } ) 
32. Bc2 Bf7 33. Ng3 Nb7 34. Bd1 $1 { The winning manoeuvre; Black cannot prevent a light-square penetration. } h6 ( { Or } 34... Qe8 35. Bb6 Rxa2 36. Rxa2 Qd7 37. Ra7 Rb8 38. Bh5 { and White wins. } ) 
35. Bh5 Qe8 36. Qd1 Nd8 37. Ra3 Kf8 38. R1a2 Kg8 39. Ng4 $1 Kf8 ( { Not } 39... Bxh5 40. Nxh5 Qxh5 $2 41. Nxf6 $1 Bxf6 42. Qxh5 { and White wins even faster than in the game. } ) 
40. Ne3 Kg8 41. Bxf7+ Nxf7 42. Qh5 Nd8 43. Qg6 Kf8 44. Nh5 { Black had suffered long enough. } 
 1-0

